# {{lb|en|zoology}} [[maintain|Maintaining]] a [[relatively]] [[constant]] and [[warm]] [[body temperature]], regardless of the [[ambient]] temperature; [[endothermic]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=&#91;{{w|George Henry Lewes}}&#93;|title=Animal Heat|journal=[[w:Blackwood's Magazine|Blackwood’s Edinburgh Magazine]]|volume=LXXXIV|issue=DXVI|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:Blackwood (publishing house)|William Blackwood & Sons]]|month=October|year=1858|page=427|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_blackwoods-magazine_1858-10_84_516/page/427/mode/1up|column=1|passage=The cold-blooded animals are all feeble breathers, and the most energetic breathers are the '''warmest-blooded'''. A mollusc, a fish, a frog, a quadruped, and a bird, represent the various stages of this correspondence.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Glover Morrill Allen|chapter=Feathers|title=Birds and Their Attributes|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=Marshall Jones Company|month=September|year=1925|month_published=February|year_published=1937|page=37|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/birdstheirattrib0000glov/page/37/mode/1up|passage=Moreover, instead of hibernating like our northern reptiles, they [birds] are able to remain active all winter, even in arctic latitudes, so long as sufficient food is available to maintain the body. This retention of heat increases the body temperature, and implies an increase as well of the creature’s activity, so that birds in addition to being the '''warmest-blooded''' animals are at the same time the quickest and most active.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Barry Eisler|title=Livia Lone|location=Seattle, Wash.|publisher=[[w:Amazon Publishing#List of imprints|Thomas & Mercer]]|year=2016|page=6|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/livialone0000eisl/page/6/mode/1up|isbn=9781503939660|passage=“It’s getting chilly,” the Asian chick said, maybe just to hear the sound of her own voice. {{...}} / “Oh, I don’t know. Feels all right to me.” / “I guess you’re '''warmer-blooded''' than I am. Could I have my sweatshirt now?”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Fiona Buckley [pseudonym; {{w|Valerie Anand}}]|title=The Heretic’s Creed|series=The Ursula Blanchard Mysteries|location=Sutton, Surrey|publisher=[[w:Severn House Publishers#Crème de la Crime|Crème de la Crime]]|year=2016|page=73|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/hereticscreed0000buck/page/73/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-78029-091-1|passage=We stood there, shivering, colder even than when we had been on our horses, for horses are '''warmer-blooded''' than their riders, and one always gains heat from them.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} {{cap|passionate}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Adele Griffin|title=Witch Twins and Melody Malady|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Disney Publishing Worldwide#Hyperion Books for Children|Hyperion Books for Children]]|year=2003|page=27|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/witchtwinsmelody00grif/page/27/mode/1up|isbn=0-7868-1940-5|passage=Claire jumped from her chair and smiled the '''warmest-blooded''' smile that she could muster.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Donald Bain (writer)|Donald Bain]]|title=A Question of Murder|series=[[w:List of Murder, She Wrote novels|Murder, She Wrote]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Signet, {{w|New American Library}}|year=2006|page=35|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/murdershewrotequ00jess/page/35/mode/1up|isbn=0-451-21817-5|passage=Chasseur said, “Murder is a grisly, nasty business, Jessica. The female species simply doesn’t have the genetic makeup to deal sensibly with it. Murder is a cold-blooded act. Women are, by nature, '''warmer-blooded''' than men, which is why they’re so attractive to the male of the species.”}}

